.TH tkutltree n 0.3 tkutils
.SH tkutils::tkutltree
.PP
Convert between nested data and a \fBtablelist\fR tree, with \fBfree column mapping\fR. A node is a dict of named fields plus an optional list of child nodes; \fB\-fields\fR maps field names to columns in order, so each tree row carries several columns — not just a key/value pair. Library\-neutral.
.SS Package
.CS
package require tkutils::tkutltree 0.3
.CE
.PP
Requires \fBTk\fR and \fBtablelist\fR. The tablelist should have a tree column (\fB\-treecolumn\fR).
.SS "Data model"
.CS
set data {
    {name Root size 0 type dir children {
        {name docs size 0 type dir children {
            {name a.txt size 12 type file}
            {name b.txt size 34 type file}
        }}
        {name img.png size 900 type file}
    }}
}
.CE
.PP
Each node is a dict: \fB<field> <value> ...\fR plus an optional \fBchildren\fR (configurable via \fB\-childrenkey\fR) holding a list of child nodes. The top level is a list of nodes.
.SS Commands
.CS
::tkutils::tkutltree::fromData tbl data -fields {f ...} ?options?   ;# -> top-level keys
::tkutils::tkutltree::toData   tbl       -fields {f ...} ?-childrenkey children? ?-node root? ?-idfield f?
::tkutils::tkutltree::clear    tbl ?-node root?
::tkutils::tkutltree::lazy     tbl loadCmd -fields {f ...} ?options?
::tkutils::tkutltree::reveal   tbl index      ;# expand ancestors, see; -> row index
::tkutils::tkutltree::findId   tbl id         ;# -> row index or -1
.CE
.IP \(bu
\fB\-fields\fR (required) — field names in column order. Field \fIi\fR fills column \fIi\fR; missing fields become empty cells.
.IP \(bu
\fB\-childrenkey\fR — the node key holding child nodes (default \fBchildren\fR).
.IP \(bu
\fB\-parent\fR\fB / \fR\fB\-node\fR — a tablelist node reference (full key) to build under / read from (default \fBroot\fR, the whole tree).
.IP \(bu
\fB\-idfield f\fR — the value of field \fBf\fR becomes the row's \fB\-name\fR (a stable id for \fBfindId\fR, \fBfindrowname \-descend\fR); \fBtoData\fR writes it back.
.IP \(bu
\fB\-expand none|all|N\fR — collapse everything, expand everything, or show \fBN\fR levels. Without it rows are inserted as tablelist does (expanded).
.IP \(bu
\fB\-lazykey f\fR — a node with a true field \fBf\fR and no children gets an expand arrow; its children are loaded by \fBlazy\fR.
.PP
\fBfromData\fR inserts each level with one \fBinsertchildlist\fR.
.SS "Lazy loading"
.CS
proc loadDir {tbl key} {
    set dir [$tbl rowcget $key -name]
    lmap f [glob -nocomplain -directory $dir *] {
        list path $f name [file tail $f] lazy [file isdirectory $f]
    }
}
::tkutils::tkutltree::lazy .t loadDir -fields {name} -idfield path -lazykey lazy
::tkutils::tkutltree::fromData .t [list [list path $top name $top lazy 1]] \
    -fields {name} -idfield path -lazykey lazy
.CE
.PP
\fBlazy\fR sets the table's \fB\-expandcommand\fR and \fB\-populatecommand\fR. The first expand of a lazy node, or a \fBsearchcolumn \-descend\fR that reaches it, calls \fBloadCmd tbl key\fR once; the returned nodes are inserted with the options given to \fBlazy\fR. Nodes returned with a true \fB\-lazykey\fR field are lazy again.
.SS Usage
.CS
package require Tk
package require tablelist_tile
package require tkutils::tkutltree

tablelist::tablelist .t -columns {0 Name left 0 Size right 0 Type left} -treecolumn 0
pack .t

::tkutils::tkutltree::fromData .t $data -fields {name size type}

# ... user expands/edits ...

set data2 [::tkutils::tkutltree::toData .t -fields {name size type}]
.CE
.PP
\fBfromData\fR/\fBtoData\fR round\-trip is stable: reading a tree back and rebuilding it yields the same structure.
.SS Notes
.IP \(bu
The number of \fB\-fields\fR should match the number of columns you want filled; extra columns are left empty, extra fields are ignored.
.IP \(bu
\fBtoData\fR reads the \fBraw\fR cell values (\fBget\fR), so it returns the stored data, not any \fB\-formatcommand\fR display text.
.IP \(bu
Use a different \fB\-childrenkey\fR if your data already uses \fBchildren\fR for something else.
.SS "Error codes"
.PP
\fB\-errorcode {TKUTILS TKUTLTREE <REASON>}\fR (\fBFIELDS\fR, \fBOPTION\fR, \fBEXPAND\fR).
